{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-749ac257a8f941b4ab171ca330d90185","ordinal":681,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.181","text":"\n\nand the like. The Stoics too say that there is a threefold class of goods: some are in the soul, like the excellences; some are external, like the excellent person and ⟨the⟩ friend; and some are neither in the soul nor external, such as the excellent person in relation to himself. But the bodily things [or external], which the Peripatetics say are goods, they say are not goods. Some have embraced pleasure as good, while others say it is downright bad, so that one of the philosophers even cried out, “I would rather go mad than feel pleasure.”\n〔1〕 The saying is usually attributed to Antisthenes, a follower of Socrates."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4818fad785254e8ab5557a2963b754aa","ordinal":682,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.182","text":"\n\nIf, then, the things that move us by nature move everyone in the same way, but we are not all moved in the same way by the so-called goods, nothing is good by nature. For it is not possible to trust all the positions set out above, because they conflict, nor any one of them. Whoever says that this position should be trusted and that one not at all has the arguments of those who hold the opposite view ranged against him. So he becomes part of the disagreement, and for this reason he will himself need someone to judge, along with the others, instead of judging others. Therefore, since there is no agreed criterion or proof, because of the undecidable disagreement about these too, he will end up in suspension of judgment, and for this reason he will not be able to affirm what is good by nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f1e0b4fcad124f1e992be8757d316023","ordinal":683,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.183","text":"\n\nAgain, some people also say this: the good is either choosing itself or what we choose. Now choosing is not good on its own account. Otherwise we would not hurry to get what we choose, for fear of losing the further choosing of it. For example, if seeking after drink were good, we would not hurry to get drink, since once we have enjoyed it we are rid of seeking it. Likewise with being hungry, being in love and the rest. So choosing is not choiceworthy for its own sake—not to say that it is actually a nuisance. For the hungry man hurries to get food so as to be rid of the nuisance of hunger, and likewise the lover and the thirsty man."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fc130464122f4b838744b8d47595b679","ordinal":684,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.184","text":"\n\nNor is the good what is chosen. For this is either outside us or in us. If it is outside us, either it produces in us a pleasing motion, a welcome condition and an agreeable feeling, or it does not affect us at all. If it is not agreeable to us, it will not be good, nor will it draw us to choose it, nor will it be choiceworthy at all. But if a pleasant condition and a welcome feeling arises in us from the external thing, the external thing will not be choiceworthy for its own sake, but because of the state that comes about in us as a result of it;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-84c221dd19d44231b3cec362ad8badc1","ordinal":685,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.185","text":"\n\nso what is choiceworthy for its own sake cannot be external. But it cannot be in us either. For it is said to be either in the body alone, or in the soul alone, or in both. If it is in the body alone, it will escape our knowledge. For knowledge is said to belong to the soul, and the body, they say, is non-rational in itself. And if it were said to extend to the soul as well, it would seem to be choiceworthy by virtue of the soul’s grasping it and the soul’s agreeable feeling. For according to them, what is judged choiceworthy is judged by the intellect, not by the non-rational body."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-071777bf0d8046d6b41cc2aec011458b","ordinal":686,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.186","text":"\n\nIt remains to say that the good is in the soul alone. But this too is impossible, given what the dogmatists say. For the soul perhaps does not even exist; and even if it does exist, it is not apprehended, as far as what they say goes, as I argued in my account of the criterion. How then could anyone be confident in saying that something comes about in a thing he does not apprehend?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1dc694afd6324f1da4412a1445fdaa06","ordinal":687,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.187","text":"\n\nBut to leave this aside too: how exactly do they say the good comes about in the soul? Epicurus, for instance, places the end in pleasure and says that the soul, like everything else, is composed of atoms. If so, there is no way of saying how pleasure can arise in a heap of atoms, or assent, or the judgment that this is choiceworthy and good and that is to be avoided and bad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f2df1447cd0e48a4b2e1d57ee85135c2","ordinal":688,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.188","text":"\n\nAgain, the Stoics say that the goods in the soul are certain arts, namely the excellences. An art, they say, is a system of apprehensions exercised together, and apprehensions arise in the ruling part. But how a deposit of apprehensions, and an accumulation of enough of them to make an art, could come about in the ruling part—which according to them is breath—is impossible to conceive. For each new imprint would always wipe out the one before it, since breath is fluid and is said to move as a whole with each imprint."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b98aee65e31c4936a5bfb358b69e9b29","ordinal":689,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.189","text":"\n\nAs for saying that Plato’s fantasy can be receptive of the good—I mean his blend of the indivisible and the divisible substance and of the nature of the different and the same—or the numbers, that is utter nonsense. So the good cannot be in the soul either.\n〔1〕 Sextus alludes to Plato’s Timaeus (35a), where the soul is mixed from indivisible and divisible being, sameness and difference; “the numbers” refers to later Platonists who defined the soul as a number."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c12199ae3bdd400eac9024d7756be30e","ordinal":690,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.190","text":"\n\nBut if choosing itself is not good, and what is choiceworthy for its own sake neither exists outside us nor is in the body or in the soul, as I argued, then nothing at all is good by nature. And for the reasons already given, nothing is bad by nature either. For the things that seem bad to some people, others pursue as good—licentiousness, injustice, greed, lack of self-control and the like. So if what is natural is by nature such as to move everyone in the same way, but the things said to be bad do not move everyone in the same way, nothing is bad by nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2c3d41d1c5c84069991dd62b9d663767","ordinal":691,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.191","text":"\n\nLikewise, nothing is indifferent by nature either, because of the disagreement about indifferents. For instance, the Stoics say that of the indifferents some are preferred, some dispreferred, and some neither preferred nor dispreferred. Preferred are those that have considerable value, such as health and wealth; dispreferred, those that do not have considerable value, such as poverty and illness; and neither preferred nor dispreferred, things like stretching out or bending your finger."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-83a5fc25a36b4a4197bca0d95d2c4e7d","ordinal":692,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.192","text":"\n\nBut some say that none of the indifferents is by nature preferred or dispreferred. For each of them, depending on the circumstances, appears now preferred and now dispreferred. If, they say, the rich were being plotted against by a tyrant while the poor were left in peace, everyone would choose to be poor rather than rich, so that wealth would become dispreferred."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-143be3fd4e264375a8458c8f18b1893d","ordinal":693,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.193","text":"\n\nSo, since some say that each of the so-called indifferents is good and others that it is bad, whereas everyone would have considered it indifferent in the same way if it were indifferent by nature, nothing is indifferent by nature. In the same way, if someone said that courage is choiceworthy by nature because lions seem to be naturally bold and brave—and bulls too, say, and some men, and cocks—we reply that, as far as this goes, cowardice too is among the things choiceworthy by nature, since deer, hares and many other animals are naturally drawn to it. Most human beings, too, are seen to be cowards. Only rarely has someone given himself up to death for his country out of foolishness, or, puffed up in some other way, seemed to do something hot-headed; the great mass of humanity avoids all such things."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b5cc98f578bf41ff89ca6aba4c2a2ec6","ordinal":694,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.194","text":"\n\nThis is also how the Epicureans think they show that pleasure is choiceworthy by nature. Animals, they say, as soon as they are born, while they are still unperverted, are drawn to pleasure and avoid pains."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0118c984d2c644c5b92e0aecc579b8fd","ordinal":695,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.195","text":"\n\nAgainst them too it can be said that what produces something bad could not be good by nature. But pleasure produces bad things, for pain is fastened to every pleasure, and pain, according to them, is bad by nature. For instance, the drunkard takes pleasure in filling himself with wine, the glutton with food, and the lecher in indulging in sex without measure; but these things produce poverty and illness, which are painful and bad, as they say. So pleasure is not good by nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ba496ee22b0d4fbc9091c1c3c4b0fa3e","ordinal":696,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.196","text":"\n\nSimilarly, what produces something good is not bad by nature; and pains bring about pleasures. For we acquire knowledge through toil; this is also how someone gets possession of wealth or of a beloved; and pains secure health. So toil is not bad by nature. Indeed, if pleasure were good by nature and toil bad, everyone would be disposed in the same way toward them, as we were saying; but we see many philosophers choosing toil and endurance and despising pleasure."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-35ebc64b329d485abfc795a0fcf8df84","ordinal":697,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.197","text":"\n\nIn the same way, those who say that the life of excellence is good by nature could be turned around by the fact that some of the wise choose the life of pleasure. Thus the disagreement among those very people overturns the claim that anything is by nature of this kind or that."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8f896322e2924ad19f22b5ef3c8ffe32","ordinal":698,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.198","text":"\n\nIt may perhaps not be out of place, in addition, to dwell briefly and more specifically on the views about what is shameful and not shameful, unlawful and not, and about laws and customs, piety toward the gods, reverence toward the dead and the like. For in this way too we shall find great variation about what should and should not be done."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d0a5b9f3f624401a8fd1e506eb1c2309","ordinal":699,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.199","text":"\n\nFor instance, among us sex between men is considered shameful, or rather actually illegal; but among the Germans, they say, it is not shameful but one of the ordinary things. It is said that among the Thebans too, in the old days, this was not thought shameful; and they say that Meriones the Cretan got his name as a hint at the Cretan custom; and some trace the ardent friendship of Achilles for Patroclus to this.\n〔1〕 The name Meriones was punningly connected with mēros, “thigh,” as an allusion to intercourse between the thighs."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f517039b246b43aaaf066becfeb517b5","ordinal":700,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.200","text":"\n\nAnd what is surprising in that, when the Cynic philosophers and the followers of Zeno of Citium, Cleanthes and Chrysippus say that it is indifferent? Again, having sex with a woman in public, though it seems shameful among us, is not thought shameful among some of the Indians. They have sex in public without concern, as we have also heard about the philosopher Crates."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c40a3c8791ff49108dde0f13bf99bd85","ordinal":701,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.201","text":"\n\nAgain, for women to work as prostitutes is shameful and disgraceful among us, but among many Egyptians it is a mark of honor. They say, at any rate, that those who have slept with the most men even wear an anklet, a token of their pride. And among some of them girls collect their dowries from prostitution before marriage and then marry. We see the Stoics too saying that it is not out of place to live with a prostitute or to make a living from a prostitute’s earnings."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ce1c64188819492dbf565e82983f9971","ordinal":702,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.202","text":"\n\nAgain, being tattooed seems shameful and degrading among us, but many Egyptians and Sarmatians tattoo their newborn children."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c1637362289f496597f7ea08a98be59d","ordinal":703,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.203","text":"\n\nFor males to wear earrings is shameful among us, but among some foreign peoples, such as the Syrians, it is a token of noble birth. Some, taking this token of noble birth further, even pierce their children’s noses and hang silver or gold rings from them—which no one among us would do,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-823e6f6583304da9b9aeae1d74e4c64d","ordinal":704,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.204","text":"\n\njust as no man here would dress in a flowered, ankle-length robe, although among the Persians this, which seems shameful among us, is thought most becoming. And when such a robe was offered at the court of Dionysius, the tyrant of Sicily, to the philosophers Plato and Aristippus, Plato sent it back, saying: “I could not put on a woman’s robe,\nwhen I was born a man.” Aristippus accepted it, saying: “For even amid Bacchic revels\nthe woman who is chaste will not be corrupted.” So even among the wise, this seemed not shameful to one and shameful to the other.\n〔1〕 Both replies quote Euripides’ Bacchae (836 and 317–318)."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f6b9fd62e7fa4014be0b17cee139e33a","ordinal":705,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.205","text":"\n\nAmong us it is unlawful to marry one’s own mother or sister. But the Persians, and especially those of them reputed to practice wisdom, the Magi, marry their mothers; the Egyptians take their sisters in marriage; and, as the poet says, “Zeus addressed Hera, his sister and wife.” Zeno of Citium, too, says it is not out of place to rub one’s mother’s private part with one’s own, just as no one would say it was bad to rub any other part of her body with one’s hand. Chrysippus in his Republic holds the belief that a father should have children by his daughter, a mother by her son, and a brother by his sister. And Plato declared, more generally, that wives should be held in common."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7b23775e6a414e3fb0dedd535f0d14f7","ordinal":706,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.206","text":"\n\nAnd masturbation, which is detestable among us, Zeno does not disapprove of; and we hear that others too make use of this bad thing as if it were something good."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-91029b9830284f2d82d8c9ed25763abe","ordinal":707,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.207","text":"\n\nAgain, tasting human flesh is unlawful among us, but among whole foreign nations it is indifferent. And why speak of foreigners, when even Tydeus is said to have eaten his enemy’s brain, and the Stoics say it is not out of place for someone to eat the flesh of other people and of himself?\n〔1〕 Tydeus, one of the Seven against Thebes, was said to have eaten the brain of his enemy Melanippus as he lay dying."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b1edbdea8edc4dbe8229af3a08ec7ca1","ordinal":708,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.208","text":"\n\nTo stain a god’s altar with human blood is, among us, unlawful in most people’s view; but the Spartans are flogged bitterly at the altar of Artemis Orthosia so that a great stream of blood may flow over the goddess’s altar. Some, too, sacrifice a human being to Cronus, just as the Scythians sacrifice strangers to Artemis; whereas we think that holy places are defiled by the killing of a human being."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8232e8c9293d44b3ae809ca72eac6a57","ordinal":709,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.209","text":"\n\nAmong us the law punishes adulterers, but among some peoples it is indifferent to have sex with other men’s wives; and some philosophers too say that having sex with another man’s wife is indifferent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-caecb5b71aed43afbc5a3d4446d24f27","ordinal":710,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.210","text":"\n\nAmong us the law commands that fathers should receive care from their children; but the Scythians cut their throats once they are past sixty. And what is surprising in that, if Cronus cut off his father’s genitals with a sickle, Zeus cast Cronus down into Tartarus, and Athena, with Hera and Poseidon, tried to put her father in chains? Cronus, too, decided to do away with his own children,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e18f58ae64ac48859d3ecee4da2d8abf","ordinal":711,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.211","text":"\n\nand Solon gave the Athenians his law on acts not liable to trial, under which he allowed each man to kill his own child; whereas among us the laws forbid killing children. The Roman lawgivers order children to be subject to their fathers, and their slaves, and the children’s property to be controlled not by the children but by the fathers, until the children obtain their freedom, as bought slaves do; but among others this has been rejected as tyrannical."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3f4a6b4a1e6e4039981fa246b3fa60ec","ordinal":712,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.212","text":"\n\nIt is the law that murderers are punished; but gladiators who kill are often even honored. Again, the laws forbid striking free men; but athletes who strike free men, ⟨and⟩ often even kill them, are thought worthy of honors and crowns."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-28bec58d1d194a8097b26aa2a1d3345d","ordinal":713,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.213","text":"\n\nAmong us the law orders each man to live with one wife, but among the Thracians and the Gaetulians (a Libyan people) each man lives with many."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dd71285ce43145858bee9acc5d747bb7","ordinal":714,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.214","text":"\n\nPiracy is illegal and unjust among us, but among many foreign peoples it is not thought out of place. They say the Cilicians even considered it glorious, so that those who died while raiding were thought worthy of honor. And Nestor in the poet, after welcoming Telemachus and his companions warmly, says to them: “Or do you wander at random, / like pirates?” Yet if piracy were out of place, he would not have welcomed them so warmly while suspecting that they might be people of that kind."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8867e0567c364f1592d4b604eb183622","ordinal":715,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.215","text":"\n\nAgain, ⟨stealing⟩ is unjust and illegal among us; but those who say that Hermes is the most thieving of gods make it seem not unjust (for how could a god be bad?). And some say that the Spartans too punished thieves not for having stolen but for having been caught."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e86c1a0b9c7d431a88775107ea6ef1d0","ordinal":716,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.216","text":"\n\nAgain, in many places the law punishes the coward and the man who throws away his shield. That is why the Spartan woman, handing her son his shield as he set out for war, said, “Either with this, my child, or on this.” But Archilochus, as if boasting to us about having thrown away his shield and run, says of himself in his poems: “Some Saian delights in my shield, which beside a bush\nI left—a blameless weapon—against my will;\nbut I myself escaped the end of death.”\n〔1〕 The Saians were a Thracian tribe against whom Archilochus (seventh century BC) fought."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e3f564fd800f46e7b7ef049078e1f6c9","ordinal":717,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.217","text":"\n\nThe Amazons even lamed the male children they bore, so that they would be unable to do anything manly, while they themselves took charge of war—though among us the opposite is considered right. And the mother of the gods accepts her castrated priests; the goddess would not have judged so if not being manly were bad by nature.\n〔1〕 The mother of the gods (Cybele) was served by the Galli, eunuch priests who dressed as women."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7fc03e547626444abd3a7f0a00af72c0","ordinal":718,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.218","text":"\n\nSo too, views about what is just and unjust, and about what is fine in the matter of courage, show great variation. Matters of piety and the ⟨worship⟩ of the gods are also full of disagreement. Most people say that there are gods, but some say there are not, like Diagoras of Melos, Theodorus, and Critias of Athens. And of those who have declared that there are gods, some believe in their ancestral gods, others in the gods fashioned in the dogmatic schools. Aristotle, for instance, said that god is incorporeal and the limit of the heavens; the Stoics, that he is a breath pervading even ugly things; Epicurus, that he has human shape; Xenophanes, that he is an impassive sphere."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-749023ce14ff4681b450de550b4f49ed","ordinal":719,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.219","text":"\n\nSome say that the gods provide for our affairs, others that they do not; for Epicurus says that what is blessed and indestructible neither has troubles itself nor causes them for others. Hence among ordinary people too, some say there is one god, others that there are many, of differing shapes—so much so that they even fall into the beliefs of the Egyptians, who think the gods are dog-faced, hawk-shaped, cows, crocodiles and what not."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3656c63bcbb446e3b6eea9606e2638ff","ordinal":720,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.220","text":"\n\nHence matters of sacrifice, and the worship of the gods in general, also show great variation. What ⟨is considered⟩ holy in some temples is unholy in others. Yet if the holy and the unholy were so by nature, this would not have been believed. For instance, no one would sacrifice a pig to Sarapis, but people do sacrifice pigs to Heracles and Asclepius. To sacrifice a sheep to Isis is unlawful, but sheep are offered with good omens to the so-called mother of the gods and to other gods."}]}